-- ============================================================================
-- PD6 ASSOCIATE DEEP SEED: Flow Assurance, beginner tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE RESISTANCE STACK. `layerResistance`,
-- `burialResistance`, `overallU`, `pipeMassLbPerFt` and `contentsMassLbPerFt`
-- know nothing about fluid, rate, time or temperature. They are geometry and
-- material properties in series, and every one of their answers exists before
-- anything flows. That is the simplest honest layer in this domain and it is
-- the layer everything above is built on: the U goes into every Professional
-- field and both masses go into the cooldown.
--
-- THE TIER IS GENUINELY SIMPLE, AND SAYING SO IS PART OF THE COURSE. Heat
-- leaves a flowline through resistances in series. They add. The biggest term
-- dominates. The shares are readable off the return. Not every module in this
-- programme hides something, and a tier that manufactured a paradox here would
-- be teaching a suspicion the code does not earn.
--
-- WHAT IT MUST ESTABLISH BEFORE ANYTHING IS COMPUTED, AND IT IS THE LARGEST
-- LIMIT IN THE COURSE. THE HYDRATE BOUNDARY IS AN INPUT. Both engine headers
-- say so in as many words: flowlineThermal says hydrate and wax boundaries
-- "are fluid properties, they come from a lab or a compositional flash, and
-- the consumer supplies them", and hydrateInhibition says it "does NOT compute
-- where the hydrate boundary is in the first place". So the whole course is
-- about arriving above a line neither engine draws, and every verdict in the
-- two tiers above is conditional on a laboratory number somebody else
-- supplied. The Associate tier is where that is said, because the Expert
-- tier's two findings are both about how little it takes to cross it.
--
-- THREE THINGS THE TIER MUST NOT LET A LEARNER ASSUME.
--
--   A U IS MEANINGLESS UNTIL YOU SAY WHAT IT IS REFERRED TO. `overallU` takes
--   `referenceIdIn`, defaults it to the pipe bore, and REPORTS IT BACK, and
--   the module header calls mixing two references the commonest mistake in a
--   flow assurance hand calculation. On the capstone stack the same physics
--   referred to the coated outside diameter is 0.3153 against 0.5428
--   Btu/(hr ft2 degF). The Expert tier prices that seam; this tier is where
--   the habit of naming the reference is built.
--
--   AN UNKNOWN CATALOG ID IS A NaN AND NOT A DEFAULT. `conductivity` and
--   `filmCoefficient` return NaN for an id in neither catalog and
--   DELIBERATELY do not fall back. The header records why: an earlier version
--   returned the first entry, carbon steel, so a typo in an insulation id
--   quietly turned aerogel into steel and made a line look two thousand times
--   better insulated than it is. "A NaN propagates into a refusal; a plausible
--   wrong number does not" is the module's own sentence and it is the
--   discipline the Expert tier then watches fail twice.
--
--   THE FILM COEFFICIENTS ARE THE UNCERTAIN INPUT AND ARE EXPOSED FOR THAT
--   REASON. Still water and a swept seabed are an order of magnitude apart,
--   50 against 200, and no correlation in the file would be worth more than an
--   operator's own measured number. On a well insulated buried line neither
--   film matters much: on the capstone stack the two of them together carry
--   under 0.7 percent of the resistance. On a shut-in line the bore-side film
--   is the one that stops being a short circuit, which is why the two
--   catalogs are kept apart.
--
-- A RESULT WORTH STATING BECAUSE THE MODULE HEADER GETS THE RANKING BACKWARDS.
-- `cooldownTime`'s header warns that "on an insulated small-bore line the
-- steel can hold as much heat as the oil in it". On the capstone line the
-- steel DOES outweigh the contents, 40.298 against 26.011 lbm/ft, and it
-- carries far LESS of the heat capacity, because 0.113 Btu/(lb degF) meets
-- 0.58: the contents hold 76.8 percent of the M Cp against the steel's 23.2.
-- Mass and thermal mass rank in opposite directions here. That is not a
-- defect, it is a reason to compute a heat capacity rather than eyeball a
-- weight, and this tier is where both masses are computed so the tier above
-- can put them together.
--
-- AND THE LAYERS THAT CARRY MOST OF THE RESISTANCE CARRY NONE OF THE MASS, AS
-- THE API READS. `overallU` accepts an unbounded layer list; `cooldownTime`
-- has exactly two mass slots, `contents` and `shell`. On the capstone stack
-- the foam and the weight coat carry 56.56 percent of the resistance and
-- contribute nothing to the thermal mass unless a caller hand-lumps them into
-- `shell`. The Professional tier owns what that does to a no-touch time. This
-- tier owns noticing that a list and two slots are not the same shape.
--
-- The only numbers that travel from this tier to the next are the line's own
-- stated dimensions and material properties. The tier above restates every one
-- of them from scratch.
-- ============================================================================

values ('flowassurance', 'beginner', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-a-line-that-cools","title":"A Line That Cools","lesson_keys":["l01-why-a-flowline-is-a-thermal-problem","l02-what-this-engine-models","l03-what-it-refuses","l04-the-boundary-is-an-input"]},{"key":"m02-resistances-in-series","title":"Resistances in Series","lesson_keys":["l01-heat-leaves-through-layers","l02-a-layer-is-a-resistance","l03-adding-them-up","l04-the-overall-coefficient","l05-what-a-u-value-is-referred-to"]},{"key":"m03-insulation","title":"Insulation","lesson_keys":["l01-what-insulation-buys","l02-the-foams-share","l03-diminishing-returns","l04-what-insulation-cannot-do"]},{"key":"m04-burial","title":"Burial","lesson_keys":["l01-a-trench-is-a-resistance-too","l02-soil-conductivity","l03-the-burial-term","l04-what-depth-buys","l05-what-burial-assumes"]},{"key":"m05-what-the-line-weighs","title":"What the Line Weighs","lesson_keys":["l01-steel-mass-per-foot","l02-contents-mass-per-foot","l03-why-mass-matters-later","l04-the-layers-that-carry-none","l05-what-a-mass-is-for"]},{"key":"m06-the-associate-reading","title":"The Associate Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
on conflict (app_slug, tier) do update
  set structure = excluded.structure,
      content_version = excluded.content_version,
      active = true,
      updated_at = now();

delete from public.academy_quiz_questions
 where app_slug = 'flowassurance' and tier = 'beginner';

